  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    What the business owns equals what it owes to outsiders plus what it owes its owner.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Capital = 500,000 − 180,000 = ₦320,000.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Profit increases capital; drawings reduce it.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Stock rises (asset) and the creditor rises (liability) by the same amount.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    One asset rises and another falls by the same amount.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Profit belongs to the owner, so capital rises.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Drawings take value out of the business.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    DEAD CLIC: debits increase expenses, assets and drawings.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Credits increase liabilities, income and capital.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Returns inwards is an expense-like account, so it is debited; the debtor's balance falls.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    The liability falls (debit) and the returns outwards account is credited.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Discount allowed is a cost of selling on credit, so it is an expense.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    The firm pays less than it owed, which is income.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    The expense rises and the debtor is removed.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Cash rises (asset) and sales is credited (income).
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    The liability falls (debit) and cash falls (credit).
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Cash rises (debit) and the debtor's balance falls (credit).
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Drawings is debited because it reduces the owner's stake.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Cash rises (debit) and capital rises (credit).
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Cash rises and a liability is created.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    A fixed asset rises and a liability rises.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    The expense is charged and the accumulated depreciation account is credited.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    The expense for the period is completed and a liability is created.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    The part of the cost belonging to next year is carried as an asset.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    It goes on the smaller side so that the two columns can be made equal.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    The balance is brought down on the opposite side to the one on which it stood.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    It is an arithmetic check on the double entry, nothing more.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Expenses carry debit balances; capital, sales and creditors carry credits.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    A provision is a credit balance, deducted from debtors in the balance sheet.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    850,000 − 320,000 = ₦530,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    530,000 − 70,000 + 30,000 = ₦490,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Cash falls and the liability falls, so the equation still holds.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    The debtor rises by the selling price and capital rises by the profit margin.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    A debit balance in an asset account is value owned.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    One asset is exchanged for another, so the total is unchanged.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    The liability falls and the returns outwards account is credited.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Double entry is the mechanism that preserves the equality.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    420,000 = 300,000 + 100,000 + profit − 40,000, so profit = ₦60,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Capital is the owner's claim on the business, which is why it sits on the credit side.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    A one-sided entry breaks the equality, which is exactly what the trial balance detects.
  ]

]

